.TH VAUTHCTL 1 "September 2026" "vAuth 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vauthctl \- manage the vAuth virtual authenticator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vauthctl
.B status
.br
.B vauthctl
.B provision
.br
.B vauthctl
.B credentials list
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B vauthctl
.B credentials delete
.B --owner
.I uid
.B --id
.I credential-id
.br
.B vauthctl
.B credentials clear --confirm-destroy-all
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vauthctl
reports vAuth service status and manages the TPM-protected credential store.
Commands that access the store are re-executed as a restricted transient
systemd service under the
.B vauth
system account. systemd supplies the encrypted database authorization
credential to that service.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Report whether the daemon is running, the state of
.BR vauth.service ,
and whether a UI agent is available.
.TP
.B provision
Create the TPM-backed database key and rollback counter required by vAuth.
Run this once before starting the daemon with a new store.
.TP
.B credentials list
List credential metadata. This does not display private key material.
.TP
.B credentials delete
Delete the credential selected by its owner UID and hexadecimal credential ID.
.TP
.B credentials clear
Delete every credential in the store while preserving the TPM security
objects. The explicit
.B --confirm-destroy-all
option is required.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Display command help and exit.
.TP
.BI "credentials list --owner " uid
Only list credentials owned by the given numeric UID.
.TP
.BI "credentials list --rp " relying-party-id
Only list credentials for the given relying-party ID.
.TP
.BI "credentials delete --owner " uid
Select the credential owner by numeric UID.
.TP
.BI "credentials delete --id " credential-id
Select a credential by its hexadecimal ID. The ID must contain between 32 and
2048 hexadecimal characters and have an even length.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/credstore.encrypted/vauth-db-auth
systemd-encrypted database authorization credential used for managed
operations.
.TP
.I /var/lib/vauth
Persistent vAuth state directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd-run (1),
.BR vauth-ui (1)
